A||||1||Cozmo||AMC Duke clone???||||1||
Z||000000||Cozmo||03-25-2001||03:37 PM||||When searching for Duke repair info I found this...<BR>The new, downsized Jeep Cherokee XJ was introduced in 1984 along with the <BR>new Jeep 2.5L four cylinder engine. Because it shares the same size with the <BR>previously used GM four it is often confused with it. The Jeep four was created by<BR>cutting the two center cylinders out of a 258 six block. A single piece rear main<BR>seal was designed instead of the 258 two piece seal and the GM 2.5L bell<BR>housing pattern was used. This saved AMC some  money as they were already<BR>casting bell housings for the GM four and having Chrysler make 904  automatic <BR>transmissions with the GM four bell pattern. An entirely new head was designed<BR>for the new engine. A carburetor was used for the first year, with throttle body <BR>fuel injection added in 1985 and direct port injection in 1991.  This engine was <BR>adopted by Dodge to replace the 2.2L as the base engine of the Dakota mid-size<BR>truck around 1990. The only AMC passenger car to receive this engine was the<BR>1984 Eagle 4WD. The last 2WD AMC passenger car was built in 1983 and the <BR>four was dropped from the Eagle line after 1984.<P>A serial number or Engine Day Build Code (see) is stamped on the engine block on <BR>a machined pad. 2.0L (121)  OHC engines have the pad located on the left rear corner<BR>of the block near the bell housing. 2.5L models have the pad on the right side just to the<BR>rear of the distributor between cylinder #3 and #4. This does not apply to the GM 2.5L<BR>engine.<P>AMC Four Cylinder Engine Chart<BR>    Size/BorexStroke (inches) 	 Years Used/Notes<BR>    121 (2.0L), 3.41x3.32	1977-1979, 80 hp, OHC, license built from VW/Audi<BR>    151 (2.5L), 4.0x3.0	1980-1983, 99 hp, purchased from GM<BR>    150 (2.5L), 3.876x3.188	1983-84, 83 hp, Eagle and Jeep<BR>			1985-1988, 117 hp TBI, Jeep<BR>			1989-1990, 121 hp, TBI, Jeep<BR>			1991-2000, 130 hp, MPFI, Jeep<P>Does it fit a Fiero??<BR>If so, MPFI 130 hp sounds pretty good to me...<BR>It's probably a more robust engine also...<BR>C02M0<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||rockcrawl||03-25-2001||05:01 PM||||The things that caught my attention were: GM 2.5 bellhousing pattern, direct port injection in '91, and 130hp. I guess if you had one it would be a worthwhile swap, but I wouldn't go outa my way to find one. They are designed for longitudinal use, so accesory brackets might not work out very well in the Fiero engine compartment. Mating the wiring and ECM of a Chrysler to a Fiero might also be a challenge.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
